egetables, whatever was in season. After breakfast, we played, then went to lunch in a children's dining room without our parents. And then we all napped in the dormitory. After naps, we always swam in the glorious Asi--" I have a picture of that "--on the Yarkon River, which I mentioned before. This was a Roman-style swimming pool left over from the Roman time. Here, I learned to swim because someone pushed me in. [LAUGHING] After dinner, we all enjoyed playtime and a second dinner with our parents." That's when we got to see our parents, was at the end of the day. "And I especially loved eating those huge green olives and rolling down the long, grassy hill. Olives were-- olive trees were abundant in Israel and still are. On Shabbat mornings, we used to climb to Har HaGilboa near to find mosaic pieces, ruins from the destruction of the holy temple, Beit HaMikdash, and pick iris and cyclamen that grew wild amongst the rocks. They smelled so good, but we're not allowed to pick them today. The last place we lived before immigrating to America was Kiryat Bialystok until my 12th birthday, when my parents gave me a going-away birthday party. We lived here for about five years in a very small home, like in Kiryat Matalon, the one before, but with a larger plot of land. We were next door to a kindergarten and not an ammunition factory. Malka told me that there were such shtiebels, shuls-- shtiebels are small homes-- and didn't remember attending shul. People just didn't attend shul in Israel. And we know shul is synagogue, and-- as we know it here. But for one actual shul, I think on Yom Kippur, my sixth grade girlfriend [?Rima?] [?Klutz?] enjoyed eating sweet ripe guavas. This is on Yom Kippur, when you're not allowed to eat. We were sitting on the cold black earth underneath the guava tree grove, and in her yard, while our parents were attending services across the street. Entering the front door, the only door of my house here in Kiryat Bialystok, was a small, hall-like