ound 1961. In our spare time, we played tennis and visited Virginia Beach and Myrtle Beach." And here's the end-- the ending, the "Like Abba, Ima read a great deal. She was always very upbeat and always smiling, even when she was sick. Abba, at 6 feet tall, towered over her. He always looked so handsome and debonair. And Ima's elegance in dress and manner matched his good looks-- Abba in clean, ironed clothes and Ima in red lipstick, hair always in place, elegantly dressed in a skirt and blouse, and a dress that she designed and sewed herself, and a pearl necklace, which I still have. She had a bird-like voice, always singing and teaching me songs in Italian, and dance steps and routines. She was very light on her feet. She never let her nails grow long or manicured them, as she was very busy with housework and caring for all of us. The house was immaculate, down to the tiled floor. You could eat off it. The food was delicious, and our clothes, which she designed and sewed, were always impeccable. Abba and Ima were top notch in every way, both energetic, a really good housekeeper, a balaboosta, and healing patience-- the model of universal parents, both keeping us healthy and happy in mind, body, and soul. No matter the challenges that life presents, it was their tremendous courage and deep beliefs that saved us from harm so many years ago." Almost done. "They taught us about the good in every person, and that we should treat every person with compassion and understanding. I remember my very dear Ima and Abba each and every day with the most wonderful of blessed memories. I miss them with each breath and with each passing moment of my life. They must be kvelling with joy for their grandchildren. I thank God for my life and my family with every walking breath-- waking breath. I'm always happy because I'm very thankful for good people today, for the courageous and kind people who took care of us and saved our lives in the most awful, brutal, and dangerous of times dur